export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"addon":"piping","name":"Piping","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"topics":[{"slug":"piping-start","scope":"addon:piping","title":"Piping: getting started","summary":"What the Piping add-on does in CadCraft, how to switch it on, and how pipework is kept in an ordinary DWG or DXF file.","section":"start","order":1,"keywords":["piping","pipe","add-on","plumbing","mechanical","steel","copper","pvc","pex","valve","p&id","schedule","drainage"],"headings":[{"id":"what-it-is","text":"What it is","level":2},{"id":"your-first-pipe-run","text":"Your first pipe run","level":2},{"id":"files-open-anywhere","text":"Files open anywhere","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-is\"\u003EWhat it is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPiping is an add-on for CadCraft that comes with it and is free. You click the centerline of a pipe run, and it draws the pipe in its real size — steel, copper, PVC or PEX, by nominal size and schedule or type from the public standards — with its elbows, reducers and tees, the valves you put on it, a slope for drains, a solid on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E tab and a line in the pipe and fitting schedule. A P&amp;ID-style symbol set comes with it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt is off until you switch it on: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd-ons\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPiping\u003C/span\u003E. Its tools are in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPiping\u003C/span\u003E section of the right panel, as commands (below), and, when you ask for it in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPiping\u003C/span\u003E, as a group at the end of the toolbar.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA drawing in inches or feet names sizes by NPS (\u003Ccode\u003E2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E) and measures in feet; any other drawing names steel and PVC by DN (\u003Ccode\u003EDN50\u003C/code\u003E), copper and PEX by their nominal size and outside diameter in millimeters, and measures in meters.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-first-pipe-run\"\u003EYour first pipe run\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIPE\u003C/code\u003E. Type \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E for the material, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E and a size (\u003Ccode\u003E2\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1-1/2\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDN50\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the points of the run. Every corner gets an elbow. Press Enter to finish.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo branch off, start the next run on the first one: the click moves onto its centerline and a tee goes there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EVALVE\u003C/code\u003E and click on a run to put a valve on it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIPESCHED\u003C/code\u003E and click where the schedule goes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files-open-anywhere\"\u003EFiles open anywhere\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA pipe run is saved as a block reference with its drawing inside, and its data kept with it under the application name \u003Ccode\u003ECC_PIPING\u003C/code\u003E; so is a symbol. Pipework saved as DWG or DXF opens in any CAD program as drawn, and in CadCraft without the add-on with nothing lost. Its solids are ordinary 3D groups on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What it is Piping is an add-on for CadCraft that comes with it and is free. You click the centerline of a pipe run, and it draws the pipe in its real size — steel, copper, PVC or PEX, by nominal size and schedule or type from the public standards — with its elbows, reducers and tees, the valves you put on it, a slope for drains, a solid on the 3D tab and a line in the pipe and fitting schedule. A P&ID-style symbol set comes with it. It is off until you switch it on: Settings › General › Add-ons › Piping. Its tools are in the Piping section of the right panel, as commands (below), and, when you ask for it in Settings › CadCraft › Piping, as a group at the end of the toolbar. A drawing in inches or feet names sizes by NPS (2\") and measures in feet; any other drawing names steel and PVC by DN (DN50), copper and PEX by their nominal size and outside diameter in millimeters, and measures in meters. Your first pipe run Type PIPE. Type M for the material, Z and a size (2, 1-1/2, DN50). Click the points of the run. Every corner gets an elbow. Press Enter to finish. To branch off, start the next run on the first one: the click moves onto its centerline and a tee goes there. Type VALVE and click on a run to put a valve on it. Type PIPESCHED and click where the schedule goes. Files open anywhere A pipe run is saved as a block reference with its drawing inside, and its data kept with it under the application name CC_PIPING; so is a symbol. Pipework saved as DWG or DXF opens in any CAD program as drawn, and in CadCraft without the add-on with nothing lost. Its solids are ordinary 3D groups on the 3D tab."},{"slug":"piping-runs","scope":"addon:piping","title":"Piping: drawing pipe runs","summary":"Draw pipe runs by material, class and nominal size, single or double line; elbows, reducers, tees and wyes come by themselves.","section":"tasks","order":10,"keywords":["pipe","run","centerline","nps","dn","schedule 40","schedule 80","type k","type l","type m","elbow","reducer","tee","wye","branch","double line","single line","elevation"],"headings":[{"id":"drawing-a-run","text":"Drawing a run","level":2},{"id":"the-fittings-it-makes","text":"The fittings it makes","level":2},{"id":"changing-a-run","text":"Changing a run","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"drawing-a-run\"\u003EDrawing a run\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIPE\u003C/code\u003E and click the points of the run&#039;s centerline. Before the first point you can change:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterial\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClass\u003C/span\u003E — steel Schedule 40 or 80, copper type K, L or M, PVC Schedule 40 or 80, PEX SDR 9 (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/addon:piping/piping-tables\" data-topic=\"addon:piping/piping-tables\"\u003EPipe sizes and where they come from\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESize\u003C/span\u003E — a nominal size: \u003Ccode\u003E2\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1-1/2\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003E1 1/2\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1.5\u003C/code\u003E), or \u003Ccode\u003EDN50\u003C/code\u003E in a metric drawing. A size the material is not made in is refused. Changing the size between points makes a reducer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESystem\u003C/span\u003E — domestic cold, hot and hot water return, sanitary, vent, storm, chilled water, heating hot water, natural gas, fire protection or other. Each has its own layer, label and 3D color.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElevation\u003C/span\u003E — the centerline&#039;s elevation at the start.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESlope\u003C/span\u003E — for drains (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/addon:piping/piping-drainage\" data-topic=\"addon:piping/piping-drainage\"\u003EDrainage and slope\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELines\u003C/span\u003E — single line (the centerline, with symbols) or double line (the pipe&#039;s outside diameter to scale).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETag\u003C/span\u003E — a name such as \u003Ccode\u003ECW-1\u003C/code\u003E, shown on the label.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-fittings-it-makes\"\u003EThe fittings it makes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EElbows\u003C/strong\u003E on every turn: 90° and 45° by name, other angles by their angle. Steel elbows are drawn with the long-radius bend of ASME B16.9 (1.5 × the size from 3/4 in up); other materials&#039; bends are drawn at one outside diameter — a drawing choice, not a fitting&#039;s dimension.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EReducers\u003C/strong\u003E where the size changes, concentric, just after the corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETees\u003C/strong\u003E where a run starts or ends on another: a tee when it meets the main square (within 15°), a reducing tee when it is smaller, a wye at an angle.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"changing-a-run\"\u003EChanging a run\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag a point&#039;s grip to move it. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPipe run\u003C/span\u003E has the tag, system, material and class, size (the whole run), elevation, slope and lines, and shows the run&#039;s outside and inside diameter. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMOVE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EROTATE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMIRROR\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECOPY\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EERASE\u003C/code\u003E work as on any object.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Drawing a run Type PIPE and click the points of the run's centerline. Before the first point you can change: Material and Class — steel Schedule 40 or 80, copper type K, L or M, PVC Schedule 40 or 80, PEX SDR 9 (see Pipe sizes and where they come from). Size — a nominal size: 2, 1-1/2 (or 1 1/2, 1.5), or DN50 in a metric drawing. A size the material is not made in is refused. Changing the size between points makes a reducer. System — domestic cold, hot and hot water return, sanitary, vent, storm, chilled water, heating hot water, natural gas, fire protection or other. Each has its own layer, label and 3D color. Elevation — the centerline's elevation at the start. Slope — for drains (see Drainage and slope). Lines — single line (the centerline, with symbols) or double line (the pipe's outside diameter to scale). Tag — a name such as CW-1, shown on the label. The fittings it makes Elbows on every turn: 90° and 45° by name, other angles by their angle. Steel elbows are drawn with the long-radius bend of ASME B16.9 (1.5 × the size from 3/4 in up); other materials' bends are drawn at one outside diameter — a drawing choice, not a fitting's dimension. Reducers where the size changes, concentric, just after the corner. Tees where a run starts or ends on another: a tee when it meets the main square (within 15°), a reducing tee when it is smaller, a wye at an angle. Changing a run Drag a point's grip to move it. Properties › Pipe run has the tag, system, material and class, size (the whole run), elevation, slope and lines, and shows the run's outside and inside diameter. MOVE, ROTATE, MIRROR, COPY and ERASE work as on any object."},{"slug":"piping-valves-symbols","scope":"addon:piping","title":"Piping: valves and P&ID symbols","summary":"Put valves, strainers, flanges and unions on pipe runs, and place a P&ID-style set of symbols on their own.","section":"tasks","order":20,"keywords":["valve","gate","globe","ball","check","butterfly","plug","control valve","strainer","flange","union","p&id","schematic","symbol","pump","relief valve","instrument","balloon"],"headings":[{"id":"valves-on-runs","text":"Valves on runs","level":2},{"id":"symbols-on-their-own","text":"Symbols on their own","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"valves-on-runs\"\u003EValves on runs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EVALVE\u003C/code\u003E and click on a pipe run; the valve goes where you click, sized to the run (on a double-line run, big enough to show past the pipe). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKind\u003C/span\u003E chooses gate, globe, ball, check, butterfly, plug or control valve, a Y strainer, a flanged joint or a union. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove\u003C/span\u003E takes the nearest one off. The run&#039;s line is broken under each valve, and every valve counts in the schedule by kind and size.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"symbols-on-their-own\"\u003ESymbols on their own\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIDSYM\u003C/code\u003E places schematic symbols for a process and instrumentation diagram or a riser diagram: the valves above, a three-way valve, a relief valve, a reducer, a cap, a centrifugal pump, instrument balloons (field mounted, or panel mounted with the line through) with their tag — letters over the number, as in \u003Ccode\u003EPI 101\u003C/code\u003E — and a flow arrow. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAngle\u003C/span\u003E turns the next symbols. Their size is set in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPiping\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe shapes follow the common conventions of these diagrams (ANSI/ISA-5.1 for instrument balloons, ISO 10628-2 for valves and equipment); the drawings are CadCraft&#039;s own.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Valves on runs Type VALVE and click on a pipe run; the valve goes where you click, sized to the run (on a double-line run, big enough to show past the pipe). Kind chooses gate, globe, ball, check, butterfly, plug or control valve, a Y strainer, a flanged joint or a union. Remove takes the nearest one off. The run's line is broken under each valve, and every valve counts in the schedule by kind and size. Symbols on their own PIDSYM places schematic symbols for a process and instrumentation diagram or a riser diagram: the valves above, a three-way valve, a relief valve, a reducer, a cap, a centrifugal pump, instrument balloons (field mounted, or panel mounted with the line through) with their tag — letters over the number, as in PI 101 — and a flow arrow. Angle turns the next symbols. Their size is set in Settings › CadCraft › Piping. The shapes follow the common conventions of these diagrams (ANSI/ISA-5.1 for instrument balloons, ISO 10628-2 for valves and equipment); the drawings are CadCraft's own."},{"slug":"piping-drainage","scope":"addon:piping","title":"Piping: drainage and slope","summary":"Slope a drain, see its fall and its elevations, and check it against the plumbing code's least slope.","section":"tasks","order":30,"keywords":["slope","drainage","drain","sanitary","storm","fall","invert","elevation","plumbing code","ipc","1/4 inch per foot","percent"],"headings":[{"id":"a-sloped-run","text":"A sloped run","level":2},{"id":"lengths-along-the-slope","text":"Lengths along the slope","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"a-sloped-run\"\u003EA sloped run\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIPE\u003C/code\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESlope\u003C/span\u003E takes inches per foot (\u003Ccode\u003E1/4\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1/8\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E0.125\u003C/code\u003E) in an imperial drawing, or a percent (\u003Ccode\u003E2\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1\u003C/code\u003E) in a metric one; \u003Ccode\u003E0\u003C/code\u003E is level. The run falls in the direction you draw it, from the elevation you give at its start. Its plan shows the slope and a flow arrow; its solid on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E tab falls with it; the schedule&#039;s run table gives its elevation at both ends.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA sanitary or storm drain drawn without a slope takes the least slope the International Plumbing Code (2021, Table 704.1) allows for its largest size:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E2-1/2 in and smaller: 1/4 in per foot (2 %);\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E3 to 6 in: 1/8 in per foot (1 %);\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E8 in and larger: 1/16 in per foot (0.5 %).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA drain flatter than that is reported by \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIPE\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIPESCHED\u003C/code\u003E. Your local code may differ: it is a reminder, not an inspection.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"lengths-along-the-slope\"\u003ELengths along the slope\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA sloped run&#039;s length in the schedule — and in TakeoffCraft — is measured along the slope: 40 ft at 1/8 in per foot is 40.002 ft of pipe and falls 5 in.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChange a run&#039;s slope in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPipe run\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESlope\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A sloped run PIPE › Slope takes inches per foot (1/4, 1/8, 0.125) in an imperial drawing, or a percent (2, 1) in a metric one; 0 is level. The run falls in the direction you draw it, from the elevation you give at its start. Its plan shows the slope and a flow arrow; its solid on the 3D tab falls with it; the schedule's run table gives its elevation at both ends. A sanitary or storm drain drawn without a slope takes the least slope the International Plumbing Code (2021, Table 704.1) allows for its largest size: 2-1/2 in and smaller: 1/4 in per foot (2 %); 3 to 6 in: 1/8 in per foot (1 %); 8 in and larger: 1/16 in per foot (0.5 %). A drain flatter than that is reported by PIPE and PIPESCHED. Your local code may differ: it is a reminder, not an inspection. Lengths along the slope A sloped run's length in the schedule — and in TakeoffCraft — is measured along the slope: 40 ft at 1/8 in per foot is 40.002 ft of pipe and falls 5 in. Change a run's slope in Properties › Pipe run › Slope."},{"slug":"piping-schedule","scope":"addon:piping","title":"Piping: the pipe and fitting schedule","summary":"Pipe by material and size with its length and steel weight, every fitting and valve, and each run's elevations — in the drawing, as CSV, or in TakeoffCraft.","section":"tasks","order":40,"keywords":["schedule","pipe schedule","fittings","valves","length","weight","csv","takeoffcraft","takeoff","pack","quantities"],"headings":[{"id":"a-table-in-the-drawing","text":"A table in the drawing","level":2},{"id":"a-csv-file","text":"A CSV file","level":2},{"id":"in-takeoffcraft","text":"In TakeoffCraft","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"a-table-in-the-drawing\"\u003EA table in the drawing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIPESCHED\u003C/code\u003E; the command line gives the totals and any drains that are too flat; click where the table goes. It lists pipe by material, class and size with its length (and weight, for steel), then every fitting and valve by kind and size with how many, and a total. It keeps itself up to date as the runs change, in the same undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPipe length is measured center to center through the fittings, along the slope. Steel&#039;s weight is the plain-end weight of ASME B36.10M: 10.69 × (D − t) × t pounds per foot (0.0246615 × (D − t) × t kilograms per meter), so 20 ft of 2 in Schedule 40 weighs 73.1 lb.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-csv-file\"\u003EA CSV file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPORT\u003C/code\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPipe schedule (CSV)\u003C/span\u003E writes the pipe (material, size, length, weight), the fittings and valves (count), and each run (tag, system, material, length, its elevation at both ends and its slope).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-takeoffcraft\"\u003EIn TakeoffCraft\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPORT\u003C/code\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPipes for TakeoffCraft (PDF with the takeoff inside)\u003C/span\u003E plots the plan on one sheet at a standard scale and puts the takeoff inside the PDF. Open it in TakeoffCraft: one linear item per pipe size with a mark along each stretch of centerline (sloped runs measured along their slope), one count item per fitting and per valve with a mark where it sits, each with its CSI MasterFormat number by system; steel pipe&#039;s assembly carries its weight. No rates: those are yours.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPORT\u003C/code\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPipe assemblies for TakeoffCraft (pack)\u003C/span\u003E writes the same items without quantities as a pack for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELibrary\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETrade packs\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen a pack file\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"A table in the drawing Type PIPESCHED; the command line gives the totals and any drains that are too flat; click where the table goes. It lists pipe by material, class and size with its length (and weight, for steel), then every fitting and valve by kind and size with how many, and a total. It keeps itself up to date as the runs change, in the same undo step. Pipe length is measured center to center through the fittings, along the slope. Steel's weight is the plain-end weight of ASME B36.10M: 10.69 × (D − t) × t pounds per foot (0.0246615 × (D − t) × t kilograms per meter), so 20 ft of 2 in Schedule 40 weighs 73.1 lb. A CSV file EXPORT › Pipe schedule (CSV) writes the pipe (material, size, length, weight), the fittings and valves (count), and each run (tag, system, material, length, its elevation at both ends and its slope). In TakeoffCraft EXPORT › Pipes for TakeoffCraft (PDF with the takeoff inside) plots the plan on one sheet at a standard scale and puts the takeoff inside the PDF. Open it in TakeoffCraft: one linear item per pipe size with a mark along each stretch of centerline (sloped runs measured along their slope), one count item per fitting and per valve with a mark where it sits, each with its CSI MasterFormat number by system; steel pipe's assembly carries its weight. No rates: those are yours. EXPORT › Pipe assemblies for TakeoffCraft (pack) writes the same items without quantities as a pack for Library › Trade packs › Open a pack file."},{"slug":"piping-network","scope":"addon:piping","title":"Piping: flow and pressure in a water supply","summary":"PIPENET follows the drawn supply pipes from the source, adds up the fixture units you name at each free end, and finds the flow, velocity and pressure lost to each fixture.","section":"reference","order":25,"keywords":["supply","water","fixture units","demand","flow","velocity","pressure loss","hazen-williams","PIPENET","PNET","static head","hot","cold"],"headings":[],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIPENET\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EPNET\u003C/code\u003E) and click the end of a run where the supply comes in.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor each free end it asks what is there: a fixture kind (\u003Ccode\u003Ewc\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Elav\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Esink\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eshower\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Elaundry\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ebath\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Edishwasher\u003C/code\u003E) or a number of fixture units (0 for nothing). The answers are remembered for that place; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset\u003C/span\u003E forgets them. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPressure\u003C/span\u003E takes the supply pressure at the source and the pressure a fixture needs, in kPa.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt prints, for every fixture, the fixture units it carries, the pressure lost from the source (friction by Hazen–Williams, fittings, and the climb or fall of the pipe: a pipe that falls along the way it is drawn gains pressure when fed from its low end) and what is left of the supply pressure. Runs that are too small for their flow (fast) are listed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDemand\u003C/strong\u003E uses a default curve from fixture units to flow that is a labelled example. Type the curve of the code in force in the engine&#039;s settings before you rely on the flows. Fitting losses are rounded defaults. Loops are ignored. Check results with your engineer. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › Supply network writes every run to CSV.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Type PIPENET (PNET) and click the end of a run where the supply comes in. For each free end it asks what is there: a fixture kind (wc, lav, sink, shower, laundry, bath, dishwasher) or a number of fixture units (0 for nothing). The answers are remembered for that place; Reset forgets them. Pressure takes the supply pressure at the source and the pressure a fixture needs, in kPa. It prints, for every fixture, the fixture units it carries, the pressure lost from the source (friction by Hazen–Williams, fittings, and the climb or fall of the pipe: a pipe that falls along the way it is drawn gains pressure when fed from its low end) and what is left of the supply pressure. Runs that are too small for their flow (fast) are listed. Demand uses a default curve from fixture units to flow that is a labelled example. Type the curve of the code in force in the engine's settings before you rely on the flows. Fitting losses are rounded defaults. Loops are ignored. Check results with your engineer. Export › Supply network writes every run to CSV."},{"slug":"piping-tables","scope":"addon:piping","title":"Piping: pipe sizes and where they come from","summary":"The pipe and tube tables the Piping add-on uses — steel, copper, PVC and PEX — and the standards they are taken from.","section":"reference","order":50,"keywords":["pipe size","nps","dn","outside diameter","wall","schedule 40","schedule 80","type k","type l","type m","sdr 9","asme b36.10m","astm b88","astm d1785","astm f876","asme b16.9"],"headings":[{"id":"the-tables","text":"The tables","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"the-tables\"\u003EThe tables\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESteel pipe\u003C/strong\u003E — ASME B36.10M, Welded and Seamless Wrought Steel Pipe: NPS 1/8 to 24, outside diameter, and the wall of Schedule 40 and 80. NPS 2 Schedule 40 is 2.375 in outside, 0.154 in wall, 2.067 in inside; DN 50 in a metric drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECopper water tube\u003C/strong\u003E — ASTM B88: 1/4 to 4, outside diameter 1/8 in more than the nominal size, walls of types K, L and M (type M is not made in 1/4 and 5/8). 3/4 type L is 0.875 in outside, 0.045 in wall.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPVC pipe\u003C/strong\u003E — ASTM D1785: NPS 1/2 to 12, the same outside diameters as steel, the standard&#039;s minimum walls for Schedule 40 and 80.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPEX tubing\u003C/strong\u003E — ASTM F876, copper tube sizes, SDR 9: outside diameter 1/8 in more than the nominal size, wall one ninth of it but at least 0.070 in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EElbows\u003C/strong\u003E — the long-radius 90° elbow of ASME B16.9 sets the bend steel elbows are drawn with.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMetric figures are the same pipe in millimeters. The add-on draws, sizes and counts from these tables; it does not choose a pipe for a pressure, temperature or flow: that is the designer&#039;s call.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The tables Steel pipe — ASME B36.10M, Welded and Seamless Wrought Steel Pipe: NPS 1/8 to 24, outside diameter, and the wall of Schedule 40 and 80. NPS 2 Schedule 40 is 2.375 in outside, 0.154 in wall, 2.067 in inside; DN 50 in a metric drawing. Copper water tube — ASTM B88: 1/4 to 4, outside diameter 1/8 in more than the nominal size, walls of types K, L and M (type M is not made in 1/4 and 5/8). 3/4 type L is 0.875 in outside, 0.045 in wall. PVC pipe — ASTM D1785: NPS 1/2 to 12, the same outside diameters as steel, the standard's minimum walls for Schedule 40 and 80. PEX tubing — ASTM F876, copper tube sizes, SDR 9: outside diameter 1/8 in more than the nominal size, wall one ninth of it but at least 0.070 in. Elbows — the long-radius 90° elbow of ASME B16.9 sets the bend steel elbows are drawn with. Metric figures are the same pipe in millimeters. The add-on draws, sizes and counts from these tables; it does not choose a pipe for a pressure, temperature or flow: that is the designer's call."},{"slug":"piping-not-built-yet","scope":"addon:piping","title":"Piping: not built yet","summary":"What the Piping add-on does not do yet, so you know before you plan a job around it.","section":"reference","order":90,"keywords":["limits","not built","missing","roadmap","piping"],"headings":[{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERisers\u003C/strong\u003E: a run stays in plan with one slope; draw a vertical run as separate runs at their own elevations.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPipe sizing\u003C/strong\u003E from flow, pressure drop or fixture units, and \u003Cstrong\u003Ehanger\u003C/strong\u003E spacing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMore materials and standards\u003C/strong\u003E: stainless steel, cast iron soil pipe, ductile iron, CPVC, HDPE, metric copper to EN 1057, and fittings&#039; own dimensions for copper, PVC and PEX (their bends are drawn at one outside diameter).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EJoints\u003C/strong\u003E (welds, solder, threads, solvent) counted in the schedule; \u003Cstrong\u003Einsulation\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EValves on the 3D tab\u003C/strong\u003E: valves show and count in 2D; the solid is the pipe.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBranches that follow their main\u003C/strong\u003E when the main moves: draw the branch to the main again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIsometric drawings\u003C/strong\u003E from the runs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESpanish words\u003C/strong\u003E for the add-on: they come with the next language update.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Not built yet Risers: a run stays in plan with one slope; draw a vertical run as separate runs at their own elevations. Pipe sizing from flow, pressure drop or fixture units, and hanger spacing. More materials and standards: stainless steel, cast iron soil pipe, ductile iron, CPVC, HDPE, metric copper to EN 1057, and fittings' own dimensions for copper, PVC and PEX (their bends are drawn at one outside diameter). Joints (welds, solder, threads, solvent) counted in the schedule; insulation. Valves on the 3D tab: valves show and count in 2D; the solid is the pipe. Branches that follow their main when the main moves: draw the branch to the main again. Isometric drawings from the runs. Spanish words for the add-on: they come with the next language update."}]};
